## Summary
`transcript_4()` at `rust/lichen-oscore/src/edhoc.rs:398` accepts the credential as parameter `_cred` but does NOT include it in the hash computation. Per EDHOC RFC 9528 Section 6.4, TH_4 MUST be computed as H(TH_3, CIPHERTEXT_3, ID_CRED_I). The credential is currently silently discarded.

## Evidence
```rust
fn transcript_4(th_3: &[u8; 32], ciphertext_3: &[u8], _cred: &[u8]) -> Result<[u8; 32], EdhocError> {
    let mut buf = heapless::Vec::<u8, 1024>::new();
    encode_bstr(&mut buf, th_3)?;
    encode_bstr(&mut buf, ciphertext_3)?;
    Ok(compute_th(&buf))
}
```

Compare with `transcript_3` which correctly includes `cred`:
```rust
fn transcript_3(th_2: &[u8; 32], input: &[u8], cred: &[u8]) -> Result<[u8; 32], EdhocError> {
    let mut buf = heapless::Vec::<u8, 1024>::new();
    encode_bstr(&mut buf, th_2)?;
    encode_bstr(&mut buf, input)?;
    encode_bstr(&mut buf, cred)?;
    Ok(compute_th(&buf))
}
```

## Impact
Both initiator and responder TH_4 are computed incorrectly — the credential of the message_3 sender (ID_CRED_I) is not part of the hash. This means the transcript hash does not bind the identity used in the handshake, violating the protocol's authentication guarantees.

## Fix
Add `encode_bstr(&mut buf, cred)?;` before `Ok(compute_th(&buf))` and rename `_cred` to `cred`.
